PLACES=[
N('atchison-county-courthouse-ks','Atchison County Courthouse, Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','civic','standing',39.56611,-95.11944,'site','view','drive','','1896–1897',
  'The stone courthouse at 5th and Parallel Streets was built in 1896 and 1897 to replace the county\'s first courthouse of 1859. The county hired George P. Washburn, who had designed the Franklin County Courthouse in Ottawa, which officials wanted it to resemble. It was listed on the National Register in 1975.',
  'A Romanesque Revival building of gray ashlar stone with four corner towers; a seven-storey clock tower rises over the front, and the main door sits in a small arch inside a large arched porch. The roof is hipped with gabled dormers. It is a working county building; the interior was not checked.',
  'A clock tower and four corner towers over the river town.',
  'County seat building at 423 North 5th Street. Look from the street; park on the streets round the square. Office hours and visitor rules were not checked.',
  [KS,W('Atchison County Courthouse (Kansas)')],brick='no'),
N('atchison-memorial-hall-ks','Atchison County Memorial Hall (Soldiers and Sailors Memorial Hall), Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','civic','standing',39.5622,-95.1242,'site','view','drive','','1922',
  'This two-storey hall at 819 Commercial Street was built in 1922 to honour the dead of the First World War and later veterans. The architect was Rudolph Meier. It is a Neoclassical building of buff brick with a full-height porch on classical columns. Travelling theatre companies played here in the 1920s and 1930s, and the meeting rooms served both the white and the Black American Legion posts. It was listed on the National Register in 2007.',
  'A buff-brick front with a columned porch and symmetrical windows. The City of Atchison says it is now home to the Atchison Recreation Commission and still used for community events. Interior not checked.',
  'A war memorial that was also the town theatre.',
  'On Commercial Street; look from the sidewalk and park on the street. Whether the hall can be visited outside events was not checked; the Recreation Commission runs it.',
  [KS,['City of Atchison: Memorial Hall','https://cityofatchison.com/location/memorial-hall/']],brick='no'),
N('atchison-post-office-ks','Atchison Post Office (1892), Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','civic','standing',39.5632,-95.1216,'site','view','drive','','1892',
  'Congress authorised this post office in May 1890 with help from Senator John J. Ingalls, an Atchison man, and work began in 1892. Willoughby J. Edbrooke designed it. It was listed on the National Register in 1972.',
  'A Romanesque building of coursed ashlar limestone with a prominent round corner tower under a conical top. A postal-location directory still lists a post office at 621 Kansas Avenue, but that was not confirmed on the Postal Service\'s own page, and the interior was not checked.',
  'A limestone post office with a round tower.',
  'On Kansas Avenue downtown; look from the street and park on the street. If it is still a post office, the lobby is open at the Postal Service\'s hours, which were not read.',
  [KS],brick='no'),
N('atchison-santa-fe-depot-museum-ks','Atchison Santa Fe Freight Depot (Atchison County Historical Museum), Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','rail','standing',39.56,-95.1268,'site','open','drive','','1880',
  'The Atchison, Topeka & Santa Fe Railroad built this freight depot in 1880 in the town where the railroad began. It is masonry of quarry-faced ashlar stone with contrasting corner blocks and window surrounds, in two parts: a two-storey office at the west end and a one-storey freight section at the east. It was listed on the National Register in 2001 as part of a statewide railroad group.',
  'A long stone freight house beside the old railroad ground. The Atchison County Historical Society runs its museum here, with a small admission charge. Exhibits were not looked at for this entry.',
  'Where the Santa Fe began, in its original freight house.',
  'Museum at 200 South 10th Street, run by the Atchison County Historical Society ($5 over age 12, $1 for 12 and under, on its page). Hours from its own page, read November 2026; it lists closed days at the holidays. Park on the street or the depot grounds; the lot was not looked at.',
  [KS,['Atchison County Historical Society: hours and admission','https://atchisonhistory.org']],brick='no',
  hours='Summer: Monday to Friday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., Sunday noon to 4 p.m. December to March: closed Sundays. Holidays closed.'),
N('atchison-cbq-freight-depot-ks','Chicago, Burlington & Quincy Railroad Freight Depot, Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','rail','standing',39.5607,-95.1152,'site','view','drive','','1887',
  'This red-brick freight house at 118 South 2nd Street was finished about 1887 and remodelled about 1943. The Register form says it is important for its link to Atchison\'s growth as a regional railroad hub. The form says nobody knows exactly when or why it was remodelled. It was listed on the National Register in January 2023 as part of the statewide railroad group.',
  'A rectangular, gable-roofed red-brick building on a limestone foundation. The street side shows the remodel: a stepped brick parapet and divided-light wood windows. Freight door openings of the 1880s remain on the other sides. Inside, wood and steel trusses and brick walls show. The form, written for the 2023 listing, gives its use as vacant and says the rail lines and the bridge to the south are still there with a few service buildings. A 2024 photograph on Wikimedia Commons is captioned as a brewery (Servaes Brewery) in the depot, so it may have a new use; that was not checked on the brewery\'s own page.',
  'A red-brick freight house on the old river-front rail yard.',
  'Vacant when the form was written; if a brewery has opened in it, its own hours apply, and they were not checked. Look from the street; the owner was not checked. Park on the street.',
  [['National Register nomination, Chicago, Burlington & Quincy Railroad Freight Depot (Kansas Historical Society)','https://www.kansashistory.gov/resource/national_register/nominationsNRDB/AtchisonCo_CBQFreightDepot_LISTED_01042023.pdf'],KS],brick='yes'),
N('harwi-hardware-building-atchison-ks','A. J. Harwi Hardware Company Building (Lofts at 832), Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','industry','standing',39.5619,-95.125,'site','view','drive','','1907',
  'The Harwi company built this warehouse in 1907 as its fourth and largest building. A. J. Harwi began a retail hardware business in Atchison in 1875 and was selling wholesale by 1880. The company stayed here until the Great Depression closed it. It is four storeys of brick and heavy timber, an example of mill-style warehouse building, and was listed on the National Register in 2021. The Kansas Preservation Alliance says it has been rebuilt as 41 loft apartments.',
  'A four-storey brick warehouse at the corner of 9th and Commercial. The rebuild kept the exposed brick walls, heavy timber columns and beams, and put in new copies of the old windows and storefront. It is a residence now, so the inside is not open.',
  'A hardware wholesaler\'s warehouse turned into lofts.',
  'Apartments at 832 Commercial Street; look from the sidewalk and park on the street.',
  [KS,['Kansas Preservation Alliance award: Lofts at 832','https://archives.kansaspreservationalliance.org/awards/lofts-at-832/']],brick='yes'),
N('amelia-earhart-birthplace-atchison-ks','Amelia Earhart Birthplace Museum, Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','landmark','standing',39.56376,-95.11449,'exact','open','drive','','1861',
  'Judge Alfred Otis built this Gothic Revival house in 1861 on a bluff over the Missouri River, and added the dining room, cook\'s quarters and back porch in 1873. His granddaughter Amelia Earhart was born here in 1897. It was a private home until 1984, when a gift let the Ninety-Nines, the women pilots\' group, buy it. It is a museum, and it was listed on the National Register in 1971.',
  'A white clapboard Gothic Revival house with a front porch and pointed windows, set up inside as a home of about 1900 with objects tied to Earhart. Interior beyond that was not looked at.',
  'Where Amelia Earhart was born, on a bluff above the river.',
  'Run by the Amelia Earhart Birthplace Museum with the Ninety-Nines, at 223 North Terrace Street. Tickets from its page: adults $12, 60 and over $10, students $8, children under 5 free; walk-in tours taken, guided tours can be booked. Free street parking on Santa Fe and North Terrace Streets, from the page. Hours read November 2026.',
  [['Amelia Earhart Birthplace Museum: hours and tickets','https://ameliaearhartmuseum.org/home'],W('Amelia Earhart Birthplace'),KS],brick='no',
  hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., last tickets 3:30 p.m. Closed Sunday and Monday.'),
N('campbell-chapel-ame-atchison-ks','Campbell Chapel AME Church, Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','worship','standing',39.56536,-95.12238,'site','view','drive','','1878',
  'This church for Atchison\'s African American community was built in 1878 of red brick in a plain Romanesque Revival style. In 1919 the outside was stuccoed and painted white, and a limestone staircase was added in the 1950s. It stands on a bluff above downtown and was listed on the National Register in 2003, for its link to the town\'s Black community as well as its architecture.',
  'A one-storey, front-gabled church with a white painted front, a limestone stair and an above-ground foundation. Interior and service times were not checked.',
  'A small church on a bluff, white over its red brick.',
  'At 715 Atchison Street. Look from the street and park on the street. Whether the congregation is still meeting and when was not checked.',
  [KS,W('Campbell Chapel AME Church (Atchison, Kansas)')],brick='no'),
N('ebenezer-baptist-church-atchison-ks','Ebenezer Baptist Church, Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','worship','standing',39.56917,-95.12444,'site','view','drive','','1911–1923',
  'Ebenezer Baptist built this church from 1911 to about 1923. The Rev. William Smothers, who was pastor from 1881 to 1913, designed it. In 1911 the church got its pipe organ with a gift from either Andrew Carnegie or John Rockefeller; both had been asked, and the congregation never learned which. It was listed on the National Register in 2005.',
  'A two-storey red-brick Gothic Revival church (from a photograph on Wikimedia Commons) with limestone around the windows, doors and buttresses, blind tracery, and stained glass in the tall windows and the stairwell, including a window for Smothers. The wood trim and ceilings inside are described as well made. Whether visitors can go in was not checked.',
  'A pastor-designed church with a mystery gift organ.',
  'At 826 Riley Street. Look from the street and park on the street. Service times were not checked.',
  [KS,W('Ebenezer Baptist Church (Atchison, Kansas)')],brick='yes'),
N('trinity-episcopal-church-atchison-ks','Trinity Episcopal Church, Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','worship','standing',39.55917,-95.11833,'site','open','drive','','1866–1868',
  'The Philadelphia architect James C. Sidney designed this church, built from 1866 to 1868. Amelia Earhart was baptised here in 1897 and went to church here as a child; she was born nearby. It was listed on the National Register in 1985 as a good local example of early English Gothic Revival.',
  'A rectangular church of coursed limestone rubble with pointed windows and doors and an open bellcote on the north gable. Interior was not checked.',
  'Early English Gothic in river-town limestone, where Amelia Earhart was baptised.',
  'At 300 South 5th Street. Park on the street. Services times are from the church\'s own page, read November 2026; the office was not called.',
  [['Trinity Episcopal Church, Atchison: weekly schedule','https://trinityepiscopal.church/?p=118'],KS,W('Trinity Episcopal Church (Atchison, Kansas)')],brick='no',
  hours='Sunday services: 8 a.m. spoken Holy Eucharist and 10 a.m. choral Holy Eucharist (about an hour).'),
N('st-patricks-church-atchison-county-ks','St. Patrick\'s Catholic Church, rural Atchison County, Kansas','Atchison','ks','worship','standing',39.46944,-95.12139,'site','view','drive','','1866',
  'Edward McCourt, a stone mason, built this church in 1866 of native limestone. The Register summary calls it a gable-front, one-storey, one-room limestone building of the kind found in almost every Kansas county, with Gothic Revival detail in the doors and windows. It was listed on the National Register in 1998.',
  'A small limestone church with pointed Gothic doors and windows. The Register address puts it on 234th Road about two miles west of US 73. Whether it is still used for Mass, and the inside, were not checked.',
  'A one-room limestone church on a country road.',
  'On 234th Road, two miles west of US 73, per the Register address. It is a rural site; look from the road and do not enter the churchyard or building without the parish\'s say-so. Who owns it was not checked.',
  [KS,W('St. Patrick\'s Catholic Church (Atchison, Kansas)')],brick='no'),
N('mcinteer-villa-atchison-ks','McInteer Villa, Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','landmark','standing',39.56333,-95.13333,'site','view','drive','','1889–1890',
  'John McInteer, an Irish-born saddle and harness dealer who made a fortune in real estate, had this house built in 1889 and 1890 by the local contractor Owen E. Seip. It is a red-brick Queen Anne house of two and a half storeys with a corner tower. After McInteer\'s death his second wife inherited it, and a violinist later owned it. It was listed on the National Register in 1975.',
  'A red-brick Queen Anne house on Kansas Avenue with a dominant corner tower. Visit Atchison lists it among the town\'s haunted-tour stops. Whether it is lived in or open, and the interior, were not checked.',
  'A red-brick tower house from a saddle-maker\'s fortune.',
  'At 1301 Kansas Avenue. Look from the street and park on the street. Do not go up to the house; its current use was not checked.',
  [KS,W('McInteer Villa')],brick='yes'),
N('price-villa-atchison-ks','Price Villa (St. Cecilia\'s), Mount St. Scholastica, Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','landmark','standing',39.55361,-95.125,'site','ask','drive','','1872',
  'The lawyer and politician John M. Price built this house in 1872; the architects were Thomas Wise and F. W. McLaughlin. The Benedictine Sisters of Mount St. Scholastica bought it at auction in 1877 and called it St. Cecilia\'s; it has been part of the monastery grounds since. The Register form calls it a good example of Italianate and Second Empire design. It was listed on the National Register in 1972.',
  'A three-storey red-brick house with a mansard roof and a four-storey square tower, on the monastery grounds. The building\'s present use was not checked.',
  'An 1872 mansard-roofed villa that a convent bought at auction.',
  'On the grounds of the monastery at 801 South 8th Street. The sisters\' own page says tours of the monastery are given Monday to Saturday by appointment only; ask there first, and do not walk the grounds unannounced.',
  [KS,['Mount St. Scholastica: visit and tours','https://www.mountosb.org/visit/'],W('Benedictine Sisters of Mount St. Scholastica')],brick='yes'),
N('mount-st-scholastica-convent-atchison-ks','Mount St. Scholastica Convent and chapel (1901), Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','worship','standing',39.5525,-95.1242,'site','ask','drive','','1901',
  'The Benedictine Sisters came to Atchison in 1863 and moved to this site in 1877. The U-shaped convent was built in 1901 when they outgrew their first building. It is brick with limestone door and window trim and Renaissance and Gothic details, and holds both the convent and a chapel the Register form calls an excellent example of Gothic Revival, with buttresses and towers. It was listed on the National Register in 1972.',
  'The sisters\' page says its tour covers the main halls and three chapels, and that guests can walk outside to a large garden, solar panels, the sisters\' cemetery and Marian statues. The chapel\'s interior was not described in a source read for this entry.',
  'A working Benedictine convent with a Gothic Revival chapel.',
  'A living monastery at 801 South 8th Street. Its own page says tours run Monday to Saturday by appointment only, through a form on the page. Do not enter without an appointment.',
  [KS,['Mount St. Scholastica: visit and tours','https://www.mountosb.org/visit/'],W('Benedictine Sisters of Mount St. Scholastica')],brick='yes'),
N('muchnic-gallery-atchison-ks','Muchnic Gallery (H. E. Muchnic House), Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','landmark','standing',39.56861,-95.11722,'site','open','drive','','1885–1888',
  'George W. Howell, a lumber dealer, built this three-storey red-brick Queen Anne house. The Register summary says local architect W. F. Wood drew the plans and builders C. W. Benning and George W. Houghton built it in 1887 and 1888. The gallery\'s own page says it was built in 1885, so the start year is not agreed. Harry E. Muchnic, who founded the Locomotive Finished Material Company, bought it in 1922. The Muchnic family gave it to house the Atchison Art Association, which has used it as a gallery since about 1970. It was listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'A fourteen-room house with a slate roof, eyebrow windows and a wraparound porch. Howell collected oak, walnut and mahogany for years to finish the inside. Art shows are held in the house.',
  'A Queen Anne mansion that is now an art gallery.',
  'At 704 North 4th Street, owned by the Muchnic Foundation and run by the Atchison Art Association; admission is a free-will donation. Hours from the Association\'s page, read November 2026. Park on the street.',
  [['Atchison Art Association: Muchnic Gallery','https://www.atchisonart.org/muchnicgallery'],KS,W('H. E. Muchnic House')],brick='yes',conf='mixed',
  hours='March to December: Wednesday, Saturday and Sunday 1 to 5 p.m. Free-will donation.'),
N('edgar-howe-house-atchison-ks','Edgar W. Howe House (1117 North 3rd Street), Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','landmark','standing',39.57444,-95.11694,'site','view','drive','','1880s',
  'Edgar Watson Howe, who founded the Atchison Daily Globe and wrote the novel The Story of a Country Town, lived here. The Register says the house was built in the early 1880s; Wikipedia says 1882. It is a two-storey brick house with a large porch and two projecting towers, and was listed on the National Register in 1972.',
  'A red-brick house (a photograph on Wikimedia Commons shows it) on a limestone base, with a big porch and a tower at each end. Whether it is lived in, and the interior, were not checked. Howe also had a later retirement house at Potato Hill outside town, which is a separate listing not covered here.',
  'The home of the Atchison editor and novelist.',
  'At 1117 North 3rd Street. Look from the street and park on the street. Do not go up to the house; ownership was not checked.',
  [KS,W('Edgar W. Howe House')],brick='yes'),
N('lincoln-school-atchison-ks','Lincoln School, Atchison, Kansas','Atchison','ks','civic','standing',39.57167,-95.12361,'site','view','drive','','1921',
  'Lincoln School was built in 1921 for Atchison\'s Black students. The city\'s schools were integrated in 1955, and this is the last public building in Atchison built for and used by African Americans. It was listed on the National Register in 2006 for that history and its part in the 1950s desegregation talks.',
  'A Gothic Revival school building at 801 Division Street. What it is used for now, and the interior, were not checked.',
  'The last school built for Black children in Atchison.',
  'Look from the street and park on the street. Current owner and use were not checked.',
  [KS],brick='yes'),
N('stranger-creek-warren-truss-bridge-ks','Stranger Creek Warren Truss Bridge (Haskell Road), Farmington, Kansas','Farmington','ks','landmark','standing',39.51139,-95.31,'site','view','drive','','1925',
  'This bridge was built in 1925 and carries Haskell Road over Stranger Creek, half a mile south of Farmington. It is a single-span riveted pony truss, 72 feet long and 21 feet wide, on poured concrete abutments. The Register summary calls it a common design that has all the elements of a Warren truss bridge. It was listed on the National Register in 2004 as one of the Kansas metal truss bridges.',
  'A low steel truss over a narrow creek, with concrete abutments. Whether the bridge still carries traffic, and the condition of the road, were not checked.',
  'A 1925 steel pony truss on a county road.',
  'Haskell Road, 0.8 mile south of its junction with 262 Road, per the Register address. Look from the road; roadside space was not looked at on the aerial, so pull off with care.',
  [KS],brick='no'),
]
